# 4. Cache Commands

The package maintains a local cache to reduce repeated downloads and processing.

## Display Cache Information

```bash
swot-wse cache
```

Displays the current cache status.

---

## Clear Reservoir Polygon Cache

```bash
swot-wse cache --clear-polygons
```

Removes all cached reservoir footprints.

---

## Clear LakeSP Cache

```bash
swot-wse cache --clear-lakesp
```

Removes all cached LakeSP granules.

---

## Clear All Cached Data

```bash
swot-wse cache --clear-all
```

Deletes every cached file managed by the package.

---

# Typical Developer Workflow

A typical development workflow consists of the following steps.

1. Configure Google Earth Engine.

```bash
swot-wse auth
```

2. Verify the current configuration.

```bash
swot-wse config show
```

3. Adjust any required processing parameters.

```bash
swot-wse config set sources.lakesp.mad_threshold 2.5
```

4. Generate a reservoir WSE time series.

```bash
swot-wse polygon \
    --lat <latitude> \
    --lon <longitude> \
    --start-date YYYY-MM-DD \
    --end-date YYYY-MM-DD
```

5. Inspect or clear cached data if required.

```bash
swot-wse cache
```

---

# Notes

* Configuration changes take effect immediately and are stored locally.
* Cache files can be removed safely; missing files will be regenerated or downloaded automatically during future executions.
* The current release supports the SWOT Level-2 LakeSP Vector Data Product. The command-line interface has been designed to support additional SWOT observation sources in future releases without changing the user workflow.
